{"kind":"request","text":"Builder: reduce Artroom test overhead by 10x. This is hugh's new highest priority, ahead of continuing declared acts, Jam-blocking spikes, Jam and docs. Remove all tests that do not prove a useful invariant, and optimize the expensive useful tests. Work in the existing gitseq system and deliver one reviewable implementation with measured before/after cost.\n\nFirst save completed output from the currently running test/mutation work, stop the remaining exhaustive sweeps at a safe boundary, and restore owned checkouts. Partial runs stay partial. Do not spend more hours finishing a blanket mutation inventory before addressing this request. The existing functionality and unresolved defects remain owed.\n\nWhat counts as useful: a test distinguishes a plausible incorrect behavior from the required behavior at a real interface or a meaningful internal boundary. It protects something the application relies on. Assertions that merely repeat the implementation, reconstruct expected output through the same algorithm, inspect irrelevant wiring, exercise unreachable combinations, or duplicate cheaper equivalent protection do not earn a place. A mock proves only its exposed boundary. Keep a compact invariant-to-witness map by group/file; this must help review rather than become another large per-case paperwork exercise.\n\nMeasure first from existing full logs where possible. The planner's approximate 85–90% figure described test-pipeline log windows across parallel workers, including compilation/orchestration; it is not an execution-time benchmark. Establish a reproducible baseline at an exact source/runner head for (a) a normal edit-to-review cycle, including compile/setup/test/rerun cost, and (b) the complete retained automated gate. Report elapsed time and aggregate worker time, with hardware, concurrency, cache/install state and executed commands. Use a small representative baseline once when existing timings are insufficient. The success target is at least 10x less overhead for comparable work, not merely fewer test names, a faster smoke subset, more workers or silently excluded suites.\n\nAudit all package tests, new declared-acts suites, retained review/regression fixtures, browser/DO/SQLite integration tests, and the mutation/verification workflow. Delete useless cases; merge or replace redundant expensive cases with the smallest distinguishing witnesses. Preserve meaningful authority, signed-intent/exact-retry, durable state/migration, replay, concurrency and application behavior protection where it is needed. A deletion or substitution gets a short concrete reason and the surviving witness if there is one. Do not retain an unhelpful test just because an earlier request demanded the entire old suite unchanged.\n\nOptimize the heavy retained tests and their harnesses. Investigate repeated Room/worker setup, crypto/credential setup, generated declarations/typechecks, nested workspace runs, real retry/backoff waits, repeated v1/v2 equivalent cases, environment creation, and full-suite execution for each mutant. Move pure checks to the cheapest correct boundary; retain actual Room/SQLite/transport cases for properties those systems alone establish. Replace wall-clock waiting with deterministic scheduling/clock/fault controls where that preserves the property under test. Reuse setup or build artifacts only with proper state isolation and exact source provenance. Select affected tests and compile scopes during development; run the final compact complete gate once at the delivered head. Keep retry/order/restart witnesses honest rather than replacing them with mocks that bypass the relevant behavior.\n\nEarlier blanket test-retention and exhaustive per-syntactic-field/guard mutation requirements in Stage2/3/4/5 and MCP must be reconciled with this newer instruction. Protect useful invariants with a minimal set of meaningful distinguishing controls; document real redundancy or unreachable/precondition-dominated cases. Do not generate meaningless tests or run every package for every mutant to satisfy a count. A compilation, setup, timeout or harness failure still does not prove a semantic invariant. Record obsolete test counts and proof obligations as superseded rather than presenting them as completed. This changes testing mechanics, not the promised functional outcomes.\n\nCurrent useful entry points: root package.json serially runs workspace typecheck/test scripts. packages/room/package.json runs Node and workerd suites; its typecheck regenerates declarations for log/client/MCP before Room source/tests. packages/room/vitest.workers.config.ts and the Stage2 branch's vitest.workers.declared.config.ts run the whole workerd glob under separate vocabularies. packages/ui/vite.config.ts gives every UI test a happy-dom environment. packages/cli/test/harness.ts creates its harness before each test. The supplied CLI retry controls have repeatedly taken about1.4seconds per dropped-reply case. These are investigation leads, not already proven optimizations. Start from the current builder heads; main e6e6782830e0ca8a68f0d11c4d4ece5e4e98c967 is still behind the acts lanes, so a main-only audit would miss the active overhead.\n\nDeliver the deletion/replacement and harness/runner changes, exact reproducible commands, the compact useful-invariant map, full before/after timings and limitations, and one exact review head. Update the existing plans/report and contributor/test guidance so ordinary development uses the cheaper method. The checker should review this first, using focused independent counterexamples and the measured compact gate, without recreating the old expensive workflow. If a particular workload falls short of10x, report its measured bottleneck and continue reducing it; do not declare the target achieved from a non-comparable workload.\n\nAfter this priority work, the standing order remains acts enabling Jam, only spikes tied to concrete Jam gaps, then Jam and full Artroom docs in parallel. Builder still judges first-Jam readiness; this request adds no separate capability gate or approval step.","body":{"conditions":"(1) All package and active acts/MCP test and mutation workflows audited for useful invariants; tests without useful distinguishing value removed, with concise reasons and retained witnesses for meaningful protection. (2) Expensive retained tests, setup/compilation and repeated verification optimized, preserving the actual boundary each invariant needs. (3) Exact reproducible baseline and final comparable measurements demonstrate at least10x lower test overhead, including normal edit-to-review and the complete retained automated gate, with elapsed and aggregate worker time and honest limits. No speedup claim from count reduction, silently skipped suites or increased parallelism alone. (4) Earlier unchanged-suite/per-syntactic-guard blanket proof demands explicitly reconciled with this newer user instruction; promised behavior remains owed, semantic failures distinguished from harness/compiler failures. (5) Exact final source/test/compiler and compact gates pass, contributor guidance and existing plans/report updated, one exact review head and independent checker review. Highest priority; preserve completed evidence and stop unnecessary exhaustive runs safely.","priority":"highest","source":"hugh's 2026-10-03 instruction: reduce test overhead 10x; remove tests that do not prove useful invariants; optimize heavy tests; builder highest priority.","target_head":"e6e6782830e0ca8a68f0d11c4d4ece5e4e98c967","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}